<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottoman wars in Europe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottoman wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> were a series of conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and European states from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> to the early 20th century. After seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1354 the Ottomans expanded through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> in the 15th and 16th centuries, while their wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> lasted from 1423 to 1718 and included their fleet's defeat at Lepanto in 1571. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> reversed a number of Ottoman gains in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> of 1683–99. The final retreat began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> of 1912–1913 and ended in the partitioning of the empire after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottoman-wars-in-europe.jpg" alt="Ottoman wars in Europe" width="330" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Geffels · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vienna_Battle_1683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Ottoman Emirate entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during a destructive civil war between John V Palaiologos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were first deployed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> allied with John VI and plundered Byzantine Thrace, and were granted the small fortress of Tzympe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>. An earthquake on 2 March 1354 drove out the Greek inhabitants of Gallipoli, and within a month Süleyman Pasha seized the site and settled Turkish families there. Didymoteichon fell in 1361, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> in 1363–64 and Adrianople in 1369, and Ottoman territory in Europe came to be known as Rumelia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> capitulated after a four-year siege in 1387. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> fell on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> succeeded him. When Manuel II refused tribute, Bayezid besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; the siege was broken when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a> and captured Bayezid himself. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg fended off Ottoman attacks for more than 25 years until his death on 17 January 1468, and the Ottomans took all of Albania after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> in 1479. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> fell with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> in 1463, when the last king, Stjepan Tomašević, was executed.</p><p>The Ottoman–Venetian wars began in 1423 and lasted until 1718, bringing the fall of Famagusta in 1571 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> in 1669. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> began in 1683 with an invasion force of 140,000 men marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. After the Battle of Vienna a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, recovering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest in 1686. In 1687 a Venetian mortar hit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> stored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and the building was partly destroyed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> ended the war in 1699.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> faced Serbian, Greek and Romanian insurrections. The Treaty of Berlin in 1878 gave independence or autonomy to the Christian nations of the empire's Balkan territories. In 1912 and 1913 the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> cut Ottoman possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> declared independence in 1912. The empire stopped an Entente invasion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1915 and 1916 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, whose outcome led to its collapse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottoman wars in Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
